    "assumptions": [
      "Published head-length endpoints are unspecified. The 508 mm dimension is interpreted as apex to lower axe corner, consistent with the photographic proportions.",
      "Only the head and upper haft are photographed. Lower haft shape, butt treatment, full 340 mm langet length and unseen ends are reconstructions; the museum does not establish whether the haft is original or a replacement.",
      "Haft depth 28 mm, square transverse section, 3 mm langet thickness, 0.6 mm plate edges and 34-to-11 mm ridge depths are unmeasured section assumptions. Neither density nor thickness is calibrated to the published mass.",
      "The reconstructed wood ends 450 mm below the apex. The plate's flat central receiving footprint meets the wood and two langets at that plane. The 58 mm plate-origin offset is the difference between this datum and the lower axe corner, not an overlap allowance.",
      "The head is one closed forged blank. Langets are disjoint material regions with complete flat seats against the wood and head. Their joints do not assert a particular historical welding or fastening process.",
      "Two visible rivet-head levels are represented on each opposing langet. Crowns are 1.5 mm high with flat receiving undersides; shanks, drilled holes and hidden fastening are not reconstructed.",
      "Both photographs are oblique opposing faces. Exact transverse ridge shape, thickness distribution and cross-section symmetry remain inferred; no calibrated axial photograph is available."
    ],
    "scope": "Complete photographed head, continuous ridged spike, axe and beak scallops, visible upper wood, opposing narrowing langets and round fastener heads. The unseen lower haft and langet ends are qualified reconstructions. Pitting, corrosion, small stamped marks and wear are excluded.",
